The five possible places for a triangle are the five sides of the regular pentagon.  
Choosing two of them gives \(\binom{5}{2}=10\) unordered pairs of sides.

Two such choices give congruent figures exactly when one can be mapped to the other by a symmetry of the pentagon (a rotation or a reflection).  
The symmetry group of a regular pentagon is the dihedral group \(D_{5}\) of order \(10\).

The unordered pairs of sides fall into two orbits under \(D_{5}\):

* **Adjacent sides** – the two triangles share a vertex of the pentagon.  
  All five such pairs are equivalent under rotations and reflections.

* **Sides separated by one side** – the two triangles are not adjacent; there is exactly one side of the pentagon between them.  
  All five such pairs are also equivalent under the symmetries.

No other distinction occurs, because any pair of sides is either adjacent or at distance 2, and the two cases cannot be interchanged by any symmetry of the pentagon.

Hence there are exactly two non‑congruent figures that can be formed.

\[
\boxed{2}
\]<|im_end|>

