The spinner has four equally‑likely directions, so after three turns there are  

\[
4^3 = 64
\]

possible sequences of moves.  
Kendra starts at the centre (the “Start’’ square has no number).  
After each move she lands on a numbered square; the numbers that can be
landed on are \(5,10,15,\) or \(20\).

To obtain a total of \(30\) after three moves the only possible
combination of numbers is  

\[
10+10+10 = 30 .
\]

Thus Kendra must land on a “\(10\)” square on each of the three turns.
The board contains exactly three squares labelled \(10\) that are
orthogonal neighbours of the centre (at coordinates \((0,2),\;(0,-2),\;(3.5,0)\)
and their symmetric counterparts).  
From the centre she can move to any of these three squares, and from each
such square there is exactly one direction that returns to the centre.
Hence each of the three possible first moves can be followed by a unique
pair of moves that brings her back to a \(10\) square on the third turn.

Consequently there are exactly three favourable sequences of moves.

\[
\text{Probability}= \frac{3}{64}.
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{3}{64}}
\]<|im_end|>

