The three small semicircles have radius \(1\) and their centers are at  

\[
-1,\;0,\;1
\]

on the diameter \(AB\) of the large semicircle (radius \(2\)).  
Thus the large semicircle has area  

\[
\frac12\pi (2)^2 = 2\pi .
\]

---

### 1.  Area of one small semicircle  

\[
A_{\text{small}}=\frac12\pi(1)^2=\frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

There are three of them, so their total area is \(3\cdot\frac{\pi}{2}= \frac{3\pi}{2}\).

---

### 2.  Overlap of two adjacent small semicircles  

Two adjacent small semicircles have centers one unit apart.  
The intersection of the two *full* circles of radius \(1\) with centre distance \(d=1\) has area  

\[
A_{\text{lens}} = 2r^{2}\arccos\!\left(\frac{d}{2r}\right)-\frac{d}{2}\sqrt{4r^{2}-d^{2}}
               = 2\arccos\!\left(\frac12\right)-\frac12\sqrt{3}
               = \frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

Since each semicircle is only the upper half of its circle, the overlap of the two **semicircles** is exactly half of this lens:

\[
A_{\text{overlap}} = \frac12\Bigl(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\Bigr)
                    = \frac{\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}.
\]

There are two such pairwise overlaps (left–middle and middle–right), so the total overlapped area that must be subtracted is  

\[
2\left(\frac{\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)=\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

---

### 3.  Area covered by the three small semicircles  

\[
A_{\text{union}} = \frac{3\pi}{2} - \left(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)
                 = \frac{5\pi}{6}+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

---

### 4.  Shaded area  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A_{\text{shaded}} &= \text{area of large semicircle} - A_{\text{union}}\\
&= 2\pi - \left(\frac{5\pi}{6}+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)\\
&= \frac{12\pi}{6}-\frac{5\pi}{6}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\\
&= \frac{7\pi}{6}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

\[
\boxed{\frac{7\pi}{6}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}
\]<|im_end|>

