The three circular arcs are each a \(60^{\circ}\) sector of a circle whose radius equals the side length of the equilateral triangle.  
Since the horizontal base of the figure is given to be \(2\), the side length of the equilateral triangle (and hence the radius of each sector) is  

\[
r = 2 .
\]

Each sector therefore has area  

\[
\text{sector area}= \frac{60^{\circ}}{360^{\circ}}\pi r^{2}= \frac{1}{6}\pi (2)^{2}= \frac{2\pi}{3}.
\]

There are three such sectors, so the total area contributed by the three sectors is  

\[
3\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)=2\pi .
\]

The three sectors overlap in the central equilateral triangle.  
The area of an equilateral triangle of side \(2\) is  

\[
\text{triangle area}= \frac{\sqrt3}{4}\, (2)^{2}= \sqrt3 .
\]

Because this triangle is counted three times in the sum of the sector areas, we must subtract it twice to count it only once:

\[
\text{area of trefoil}= 2\pi - 2\bigl(\sqrt3\bigr)=2\pi-2\sqrt3 .
\]

Hence the area of the trefoil whose horizontal base has length \(2\) is  

\[
\boxed{2\pi-2\sqrt3}.
\]<|im_end|>

