### 1.  Equation of the upper external tangent  

Let the upper common external tangent be \(y=mx+c\) (it is above the \(x\)-axis, so \(c>0\)).  
The distance from a point \((x_0,y_0)\) to this line is  

\[
\frac{|mx_0-y_0+c|}{\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.
\]

For the line to be tangent to the small circle we need  

\[
\frac{c}{\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=2\qquad (1)
\]

and for it to be tangent to the large circle  

\[
\frac{6m+c}{\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=4\qquad (2)
\]

(dividing (2) by (1) gives \(\displaystyle\frac{6m+c}{c}=2\Rightarrow c=6m\)).  
Substituting \(c=6m\) into (1),

\[
\frac{6m}{\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=2\Longrightarrow 36m^{2}=4(1+m^{2})
\Longrightarrow 32m^{2}=4\Longrightarrow m^{2}=\frac18 .
\]

Take the positive root (the upper tangent):

\[
m=\frac{1}{2\sqrt2},\qquad 
c=6m=\frac{3}{\sqrt2}= \frac{3\sqrt2}{2}.
\]

Hence the upper tangent is  

\[
y=\frac{1}{2\sqrt2}\,x+\frac{3\sqrt2}{2}.
\]

---

### 2.  Points of tangency  

*For the small circle* \(x^{2}+y^{2}=4\).  
Substituting the line gives  

\[
x^{2}+\Bigl(\frac{x}{2\sqrt2}+\frac{3\sqrt2}{2}\Bigr)^{2}=4
\Longrightarrow x^{2}+ \frac{x^{2}}{8}+ \frac{3x}{2}+ \frac{9}{2}=4 .
\]

Simplifying yields \(9x^{2}+12x-8=0\), whose solutions are  

\[
x=\frac{-2\pm2\sqrt{10}}{3}.
\]

The point on the left side of the circle is  

\[
A\Bigl(\frac{-2-2\sqrt{10}}{3},\;
\frac{2\sqrt{10}-2}{3}\Bigr).
\]

*For the large circle* \((x-6)^{2}+y^{2}=16\).  
Using the same line we obtain  

\[
9x^{2}+12x-8=0,
\]

so the solution on the right side of that circle is  

\[
D\Bigl(\frac{-2+2\sqrt{10}}{3},\;
\frac{2+2\sqrt{10}}{3}\Bigr).
\]

Because the configuration is symmetric about the \(x\)-axis,  

\[
B=\bigl(x_A,-y_A\bigr),\qquad 
C=\bigl(x_D,-y_D\bigr).
\]

Numerically  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A&\approx(-2.1055,\;1.5635), &
B&\approx(-2.1055,\;-1.5635),\\
D&\approx(2.4389,\;2.9270), &
C&\approx(2.4389,\;-2.9270).
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 3.  Area of the hexagon \(AOBCPD\)

The vertices in order are  

\[
A,\;O,\;B,\;C,\;P,\;D .
\]

Using the shoelace formula:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum x_i y_{i+1}&=0+0+(-2.1055)(-2.9270)+2.4389\cdot0+6\cdot1.5635+(-2.1055)(0)\\
&=6.1661+9.2790+9.3210=24.7661,\\[2mm]
\sum y_i x_{i+1}&=0+(-2.1055)(2.4389)+(-1.5635)(6)+(-2.9270)(-2.1055)+0\cdot(-2.1055)+1.5635\cdot0\\
&=-5.1362-9.3705+6.1661+0+0=-8.3406 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
\text{Area}= \frac12\Bigl|\;24.7661-(-8.3406)\Bigr|
           =\frac12(33.1067)=16.5533.
\]

---

### 4.  Exact value  

Write the coordinates exactly:

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_A&=\frac{-2-2\sqrt{10}}{3},\qquad 
y_A=\frac{2\sqrt{10}-2}{3},\\
x_D&=\frac{-2+2\sqrt{10}}{3},\qquad 
y_D=\frac{2+2\sqrt{10}}{3}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Plugging these into the shoelace sum gives

\[
\text{Area}= \frac12\Bigl(12\sqrt{10}+12\Bigr)=6(\sqrt{10}+1).
\]

