The 48 congruent parallelograms tile the interior of the hexagon.  
Each interior edge is shared by two parallelograms, while every edge on the
boundary belongs to exactly one parallelogram.  

Let  

* \(E_i\) = number of interior edges,  
* \(E_b\) = number of boundary edges.

Counting edge‑incidences gives  

\[
4\cdot 48 = 2E_i + E_b .
\]

Each interior edge contributes two incidences, each boundary edge one.  
Since the interior edges are shared, \(E_i = \dfrac{4\cdot48 - E_b}{2}\).

The outer shape is a hexagon, so it has six sides.  
Each side of the hexagon is a straight chain of edges of the same length,
either the long side \(62\) ft or the short side \(20\) ft of a
parallelogram.  
Thus each side contributes the same number of edges, say \(k\) long edges
and \(k\) short edges.  Hence the total number of boundary edges is  

\[
E_b = 6k .
\]

Substituting into the edge‑incidence equation:

\[
4\cdot48 = 2\Bigl(\frac{4\cdot48 - 6k}{2}\Bigr) + 6k
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
6k = 6\cdot4 = 24 .
\]

Thus \(k=4\); each side of the hexagon consists of four long edges
(\(62\) ft) and four short edges (\(20\) ft).  

Therefore the length of one side of the hexagon is  

\[
4\cdot62 + 4\cdot20 = 4(62+20)=4\cdot82 = 328\text{ ft}.
\]

The hexagon has six such sides, so its perimeter is  

\[
6 \times 328 = 1968\text{ ft}.
\]

\[
\boxed{1968}
\]<|im_end|>

